    39  /**
    40   * Rate limiting filter, with configurable threshold above which 
non-privileged clients will be
    41   * dropped. This feature enables cutting off non-standard and 
potentially harmful clients in case of
    42   * system overload. Since it is critical to always allow client 
registrations and heartbeats into
    43   * the system, which at the same time are relatively cheap operations, 
the rate limiting is applied
    44   * only to full and delta registry fetches. Furthermore, since delta 
fetches are much smaller than
    45   * full fetches, and if not served my result in following full registry 
fetch from the client, they
    46   * have relatively higher priority. This is implemented by two parallel 
rate limiters, one for
    47   * overall number of full/delta fetches (higher threshold) and one for 
full fetches only (low
    48   * threshold).
    49   *
    50   * <p>The client is identified by {@link 
AbstractEurekaIdentity#AUTH_NAME_HEADER_KEY} HTTP header
    51   * value. The privileged group by default contains:
    52   *
    53   * <ul>
    54   *   <li>{@link EurekaClientIdentity#DEFAULT_CLIENT_NAME} - standard 
Java eureka-client.
    55   *       Applications using this client automatically belong to the 
privileged group.
    56   *   <li>{@link 
com.netflix.eureka.EurekaServerIdentity#DEFAULT_SERVER_NAME} - connections from 
peer
    57   *       Eureka servers (internal only, traffic replication)
    58   * </ul>
    59   *
    60   * It is possible to turn off privileged client filtering via {@link
    61   * EurekaServerConfig#isRateLimiterThrottleStandardClients()} property.
    62   *
    63   * <p>Rate limiting is not enabled by default, but can be turned on via 
configuration. Even when
    64   * disabled, the throttling statistics are still counted, although on a 
separate counter, so it is
    65   * possible to measure the impact of this feature before activation.
    66   *
    67   * <p>Rate limiter implementation is based on token bucket algorithm. 
There are two configurable
    68   * parameters:
    69   *
    70   * <ul>
    71   *   <li>burst size - maximum number of requests allowed into the system
as a burst
    72   *   <li>average rate - expected number of requests per second
    73   * </ul>
    74   *
